In the time before time, there lived a weaver named Selene who dwelt at the edge of the world. She was not content with ordinary thread, for her heart yearned to create something eternal.

One night, she climbed the highest mountain and reached into the darkness above. Her fingers closed around something cold and bright—a beam of starlight. She pulled it down like silk from a spindle.

Night after night, Selene gathered starlight, weaving it into a great tapestry. She wove the patterns of seasons, the paths of migrating birds, the cycles of planting and harvest. Each thread hummed with silver fire.

When her work was complete, she held up the tapestry, and it was so beautiful that the sky itself wept. The tears became the first rain, blessing the earth below.

But Selene knew her creation was too precious for one person to hold. So she released it back to the heavens, where it scattered into countless pieces. Each fragment became a constellation, a map for travelers, a story for dreamers.

